<%#
  The bar that appears when rows are selected, naming the count and offering what can be done to
  them. It **floats over the list** rather than sitting in it: Linear, Notion, Airtable and Google
  Drive all do this, and the reason all four do is the one that matters here -- selecting happens
  while scrolling.

  Locals: `actions` is required -- an array of {label:, path:, icon:}.

  **The filters stay put and stay reachable.** The first version replaced the filter row, on the
  strength of Carbon's `TableBatchActions` -- but Carbon is the only one of the systems above that
  takes the filters away. GitHub's batch header replaces a *list header* that sits below its
  filters; Gmail's action toolbar is not its search bar. Reported, correctly: "the filter and the
  picklist action are not simultaneously present... they should not be mutually exclusive."

  **And it floats rather than being inserted.** `/requests` is 1,289px against a 900px viewport, so
  a bar pinned to the top of the list scrolls out of reach exactly when you finish choosing. The
  argument originally given against inserting a row -- that it moves the table -- was wrong on its
  own terms: `bin/design/layout-shift-audit.js` skips shifts the reader caused, because opening a
  disclosure is supposed to move what is below it.

  `bottom-12`, not `bottom-6`: the table rail occupies the bottom 24px of the viewport on an
  overflowing table -- measured at 876-900 in a 900px window -- and at `bottom-6` the pill's own
  bottom edge landed on exactly 876, sitting on the rail. `z-40` clears the rail's `z-20` as well.

  Dark, which is the one place this app uses an inverse surface. A floating bar has to read as
  *over* the page rather than part of it, it exists only while something is selected, and every
  system that floats one makes it dark for the same reason.
%>
<div class="pointer-events-none fixed inset-x-0 bottom-12 z-40 flex justify-center px-4"
     hidden
     data-table-selection-target="bar">
  <div class="pointer-events-auto flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 rounded-full bg-slate-900 px-4 py-2.5 shadow-xl"
       role="toolbar"
       aria-label="Actions for the selected rows">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-white"
       aria-live="polite"
       data-table-selection-target="count">0 selected</p>

    <span class="text-slate-600" aria-hidden="true">|</span>

    <% actions.each do |action| %>
      <%#
        An ordinary link. The chosen ids are appended to its query string by the controller, so a
        batch action is a navigation like any other -- no fetch, no form, and it opens in a new tab
        if that is what the reader asks for.
      %>
      <%= link_to action[:path],
            class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-slate-600 px-3.5 py-1.5 " \
                   "text-sm font-medium text-slate-100 transition-colors hover:bg-slate-800 " \
                   "focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-white " \
                   "aria-disabled:pointer-events-none aria-disabled:opacity-60",
            data: {table_selection_target: "action"} do %>
        <i class="<%= action[:icon] %>" aria-hidden="true"></i><%= action[:label] %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>

    <button type="button"
            class="rounded-full px-3 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-slate-300 transition-colors hover:bg-slate-800 hover:text-white focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-white"
            data-action="table-selection#clear">Cancel</button>
  </div>
</div>
